::GEO_U06_L06_GP01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_GP01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A figure is enlarged by scale factor 3. How does its area change? {
=A. It becomes 9 times as large#Area changes by k&sup2;, so 3&sup2;=9.
~B. It becomes 3 times as large#That is the length effect, not the area effect.
~C. It becomes 27 times as large#That is the volume effect.
~D. It becomes 6 times as large#Doubling the scale factor is not the area rule.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_GP02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A solid is enlarged by scale factor 2. How does its volume change? {
~A. It becomes 4 times as large#That is the area or surface area effect.
=B. It becomes 8 times as large#Volume changes by k&sup3;, so 2&sup3;=8.
~C. It becomes 2 times as large#That is the length effect.
~D. It becomes 6 times as large#This does not use k&sup3;.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_GP03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>If the linear scale factor is 5, what is the surface area scale factor? {
~A. 5#That is the linear effect.
~B. 10#This doubles the scale factor instead of squaring it.
=C. 25#Surface area changes by k&sup2;, so 5&sup2;=25.
~D. 125#That is the volume effect.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_GP04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>A model car has length scale factor <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>. What is the volume scale factor? {
~A. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>#That is the linear scale factor.
~B. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>8</sub>#This does not cube the scale factor.
~C. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>16</sub>#That is the area scale factor.
=D. <sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>64</sub>#Volume changes by k&sup3;, so (<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>4</sub>)&sup3;=<sup>1</sup>&frasl;<sub>64</sub>.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U06_L06_GP05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.SIM.4<br><br>Question:<br>Why does volume use k&sup3; instead of k&sup2;? {
=A. Volume has three dimensions, so the scale factor applies to length, width, and height#Cubing matches three-dimensional measurement.
~B. Volume is always larger than area#The rule is based on dimension, not always on size.
~C. Volume uses square units#Volume uses cubic units.
~D. The exponent is chosen by the shape name#The exponent depends on measurement dimension.
}
